<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Metic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as metoikos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A metic was a free resident of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, or of some other Greek cities, without citizenship there: an immigrant from another polis or a freed slave, allowed to stay indefinitely without political rights. Metics served in the army and, if wealthy, funded liturgies, but could not own land or a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> without a special grant. They paid the metoikion, twelve drachmas a year for a man and his household and six for an independent woman, and a metic convicted of having no citizen sponsor was sold as a slave.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Foreign migration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> goes back to the archaic period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> was said to have offered citizenship to foreigners who would settle and practise a craft, but metic status did not yet exist in his time. Scholars have dated its development to the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a> in 508 BCE, but the growth of the Athenian population after 480 BCE suggests that immigrants could still become citizens then.</p><p>Rebecca Futo Kennedy places the origin of the status in the 460s BCE, and James Watson in 451 BCE, the year in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> introduced his citizenship law. The first known use of the word metoikos is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>' Persians of 472 BCE, though Watson argues that there it means only 'immigrant'. The word is formed from the Greek for 'change' and 'dwelling'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>One estimate puts the male metic population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> at about 25,000 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> began in 431 BCE, roughly a third of the total. Most metics probably came from nearby cities to seek economic opportunities or to flee persecution, though some came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>. Citizenship was a matter of inheritance, so a metic could be the descendant of an immigrant many generations back. Metics had no part in political life but might be fully integrated in the city's social and economic life.</p><p>Metics were barred from the assembly and exempt from jury service, but they had the same access to the courts as citizens and could prosecute and be prosecuted. Unlike citizens, they could be made to undergo judicial torture, and the penalties for killing them were lighter. A metic could be enslaved for failing to pay the metoikion or to name a sponsor, or for marrying a citizen or claiming to be one. Citizenship was rarely granted; more common was isoteleia, 'equal rights', which freed a metic from the usual liabilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The status lost its distinctive legal force in the 4th century BCE, when metics were allowed to act in court without a sponsor, and ended in Hellenistic Athens, when the purchase of citizenship became frequent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-phalerum">Demetrius Phalereus</a> of about 317 BCE counted 21,000 citizens, 10,000 metics and 400,000 slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, Aspasia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysias">Lysias</a> are among the metics named. In modern French the word métèque was revived as a xenophobic term for immigrants, popularised in the late 19th century by the nationalist writer Charles Maurras.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Metic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Classical Attica probably held 250,000–300,000 people, of whom perhaps 30,000–60,000 were adult male citizens; the rest were citizens' families, resident foreigners known as <b>metics</b>, and a large enslaved population that worked in households, workshops and the silver mines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Below the citizens lived the <b>metics</b>, free foreigners and freed slaves who paid a special tax and had no vote, and at the bottom the enslaved, perhaps a quarter to a third of the people or more.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
